{"name":"Venetian Plaster","hex":"#e0a28d","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"venetian","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Ellipsis of Venetian blind or alternative letter-case form of Venetian."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/venetian"},{"term":"plaster","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A paste applied to the skin for healing or cosmetic purposes.","A small adhesive bandage to cover a minor wound; a sticking plaster."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover or coat something with plaster; to render.","To apply a plaster to."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/plaster"}],"entries":[{"title":"Polished plaster","blocks":[{"text":"Polished plaster is a term for the finish of some plasters and for the description of new and updated forms of traditional Italian plaster finishes. The term covers a whole range of decorative plaster finishes, from the very highly polished Venetian plaster and Marmorino to the rugged look of textured polished plasters. Polished plaster itself tends to consist of slaked lime, marble dust, and/or marble chips, which give each plaster its distinctive look. A lime-based polished plaster may contain over 40% of marble powder."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polished_plaster","wikidata":"Q1185290"},{"title":"Plaster","blocks":[{"text":"Plaster is a building material typically used for cladding on interior walls, pool interiors, ceilings and for moulding and casting decorative elements. In English, \"plaster\" usually means a material used for the interiors of buildings, while \"render\" commonly refers to external applications. The term stucco refers to a physically different mixture commonly used for exterior building wall cladding."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Fontainebleau_escalier_roi5.jpg/330px-Fontainebleau_escalier_roi5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plaster","wikidata":"Q274988"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Mud_plaster_over_straw_bales_wall.jpg/500px-Mud_plaster_over_straw_bales_wall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Mud_plaster_over_straw_bales_wall.jpg/500px-Mud_plaster_over_straw_bales_wall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Applying mud plaster to an outside wall","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMud_plaster_over_straw_bales_wall.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Clay_plaster_ceiling.jpg/500px-Clay_plaster_ceiling.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Clay_plaster_ceiling.jpg/1280px-Clay_plaster_ceiling.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Clay_plaster_ceiling.jpg/500px-Clay_plaster_ceiling.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Clay plaster base coat on split oak lath held in place with straw and manure, covered with a lime plaster top coat, Old Economy Village, Pennsylvania (1827)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AClay_plaster_ceiling.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Clay%2C_straw_lime_on_wall_lath_closeup.jpg/500px-Clay%2C_straw_lime_on_wall_lath_closeup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Clay%2C_straw_lime_on_wall_lath_closeup.jpg/1280px-Clay%2C_straw_lime_on_wall_lath_closeup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Clay%2C_straw_lime_on_wall_